            <td height="22"> 
              <div align="center"><font face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" size="5" align="center"><b>Yet 
                another Sharon outrage -- how will media cover it?</b></font></div>
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr align="left" valign="top"> 
            <td height="2"> 
              <hr height="1" width="100%">
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr align="left" valign="top"> 
            <td height="1128"> 
              <p><font face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"><b>PMWATCH 
                - JULY 23, 2002</b> -- Today's raid deep in Gaza and the killing 
                of a dozen people, allegedly including a high level Hamas leader, 
                and the wounding of 150 innocent Palestinians, is nothing but 
                the latest proof (as though any were needed) that Sharon is intent 
                on keeping this conflict bogged down in its current quagmire. 
                Of course, for anyone with the slightest sense of recent history 
                and context knows that there is nothing new or unexpected in this: 
                whenever any movement towards anything resembeling peace or reconcilliation 
                begins to stir, Ariel Sharon unleashes his death machine and perpetrates 
                an atrocity that is sure to usher in another cycle of bloodier 
                violence. Back in December, as Arafat managed to keep Hamas from 
                striking for three weeks in a row (a small miracle in itself), 
                Sharon decided that things were getting a bit too quiet and so 
                went ahead and assassinated Mahmud Abu Hunud, a top Hamas leader. 
                A week later, two suicide bombings struck Jerusalem and Haifa. 
                Another example: back in late March, Arab leaders meeting in Beirut 
                offered full a peace plan and eventually normalization with Israel. 
                How did Sharon react? With the re-invasion of the West Bank and 
                the war crimes of Jenin, resulting in the killing of hundreds 
                of Palestinians. Dozens of Israelis were subsequently killed in 
                suicide bombings, as promised by Hamas in retaliation for the 
                invasion. And now, the same story: Hamas signals that it could 
                negotiate a cease fire, and so Sharon, true to his strategy, reacts 
                as expected. </font> 
              </p><p><font face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"> Now, something 
                worth noting in all of this is how the media plays these stories: 
                back in December, when Sharon shattered the fragile unilateral 
                cease-fire adopted by Hamas, the media's initial reaction was 
                dismay. I remember a couple of CNN reporters shaking their heads 
                over Sharon's provocative acts and commenting how this was coming 
                at a time when it looked like things were quieting down. But then, 
                the Israeli propaganda machine decended, with their spin and well-coordinated 
                message, and went to work: repeating their usual mantra, again 
                and again, drawing parallels with 9/11, demonizing Arafat, and 
                doing all they could to contain the outrage, at least until the 
                next suicide bombing took place, at which point they just switched 
                to cruise control and let CNN and its cohorts do their usual reporting/mourning. 
                The exact same script is now unfolding. Reporters on the ground 
                are reacting with dismay over this naked act of provocation, especially 
                in light of the latest signals from Hamas. They are also dismayed 
                over the toll on civilians. But keep an eye on how the story will 
                be spun and weaved in the next couple of days by the usual spinmeisters, 
                and watch how slowly the debate will be shifted away from Sharon's 
                provocative actions and his strategy of upsetting any progress 
                towards a negotiated settlement, and towards a new debate about 
                perhaps newly dug up "evidence" linking Arafat with 
                Hamas, or maybe more new pictures of "militant babies", 
                or how Arafat is failing in his attempts to bring about political 
                reforms to the Palestinian Authority.</font> 
              </p><p><font face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif">Also, keep 
                an eye on the following for your paper: (1) Did the carnage make 
                the front page? (2) Was there a picture of the death and destruction? 
                (3) Was the picture above or below the fold? (For an example of 
                the extent to which the media has downplayed Palestinian suffering 
                and highlighted Israeli suffering, see our <a href="/sites/pmwatch.org/pmw/reports/pi/frontpage/frontpage.html">report 
                on the Philadelpha Inquirer</a>, where we show that the ratio 
                of above the fold from page images showing Israeli suffering vs 
                Palestinian suffering runs about 9 to 1.</font>) <font face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif">(4) 
                What kind of a headline was run: does it focus on the death and 
                destruction or does it focus on the alleged Hamas leader?</font>
              </p><p><font face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif">Please consider 
                making a phone call. For contact info, see: <a href="/sites/pmwatch.org/pmw/contact/media.html">http://www.pmwatch.org/pmw/contact/media.html</a></font>
              </p><p><font face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif">Your letter 
                will be sent to:</font> 48hours@cbsnews.com 60@cbsnews.com am@cnn.com 
                andyr@nytimes.com bdold@tribune.com cable@msnbc.com cdavis@phillynews.com 
                Christiane.Amanpour2@cnn.com cmcmahon@tribune.com community@cnn.com 
                community@cnn.com csatullo@phillynews.com dan.fisher@msnbc.com 
                diehlj@washpost.com eason.jordan@turner.com editor@reuters.com 
                editor@usatoday.com evening@cbsnews.com feedback@thewire.ap.org 
                foreign@phillynews.com foreign@washpost.com friends@foxnews.com 
                hraines@nytimes.com inquirer.letters@phillynews.com jacoby@globe.com 
                kingc@washpost.com letter.editor@edit.wsj.com letter@globe.com 
                letters@iht.com letters@latimes.com letters@nytimes.com letters@time.com 
                letters@washpost.com letters@washtimes.com mcooke@suntimes.com 
                michael.moran@msnbc.com mmatza@phillynews.com nightline@abcnews.com 
                Nightly@NBC.com nwarwick@phillynews.com nytnews@nyt.com ombudsman@npr.org 
                oreilly@foxnews.com oregion@msnbc.com rick.davis@turner.com Speakout@foxnews.com 
                srowley@tribune.com tauth@phillynews.com tom.johnson@turner.com 
                trubin@phillynews.com Viewerservices@foxnews.com World@MSNBC.com 
            </p></td>
